WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.200
All right.

00:04.200 --> 00:06.680
So now let's check the syntax of the Columnstore index.

00:06.680 --> 00:08.600
Well it is really easy what you can do.

00:08.640 --> 00:15.400
We can just put a columnstore keyword between the clustered or non-clustered and the index.

00:15.400 --> 00:20.560
So once you specify that then you are telling SQL you want to create a columnstore index.

00:20.560 --> 00:22.600
And the rest it's going to stay as it is.

00:22.640 --> 00:26.360
Now if you want to create raw columnstore, then you don't have to specify anything.

00:26.360 --> 00:28.760
There is no keyword for the raw store.

00:28.800 --> 00:33.000
So as we learned before, we can go and create a non-clustered index and clustered index.

00:33.000 --> 00:38.440
And both of those syntaxes are going to tell SQL we are creating row store index.

00:38.440 --> 00:44.280
But if you go and use the column store keyword, then you are telling SQL that you want to create either

00:44.280 --> 00:46.840
clustered or non-clustered Columnstore index.

00:46.840 --> 00:48.680
And here there is like a syntax rule.

00:48.680 --> 00:55.160
If you are creating a clustered column index, then you must not specify anything for the columns.

00:55.160 --> 01:01.010
So you cannot go and specify anything like an ID or country or any columns over here because it makes

01:01.010 --> 01:01.770
no sense.

01:01.770 --> 01:07.930
Once you say clustered column store, then all the columns are going to be included in the new structure.

01:07.930 --> 01:11.090
So this is the syntax of the column store index.

01:11.130 --> 01:11.490
All right.

01:11.490 --> 01:12.330
So back to SQL.

01:12.330 --> 01:14.770
Let's check how we can create column store index.

01:14.770 --> 01:20.490
Now if you check our table here DB customers that we have created previously and we go to the indexes,

01:20.490 --> 01:23.050
you can see that we have created few indexes.

01:23.050 --> 01:25.650
And one of them is the clustered index.

01:25.970 --> 01:28.890
This one is a row store index.

01:28.890 --> 01:31.770
So our table is splitted by the rows.

01:31.810 --> 01:33.170
Now let's go and change that.

01:33.170 --> 01:37.250
Let's make our table splitted by the columns using the column store.

01:37.290 --> 01:43.690
So we're going to say Create clustered column store index.

01:43.690 --> 01:48.210
And we're going to give it a name index DB customers.

01:48.250 --> 01:53.250
And it's going to be on the table sales DB customers.

01:53.690 --> 01:57.370
And here if you go and specify a column it's going to be a mistake.

01:57.370 --> 01:59.010
So let's go and check that.

01:59.050 --> 02:05.490
So if we go and execute it, it says it fails because key lists or the columns is not allowed.

02:05.490 --> 02:06.770
So we cannot have this.

02:06.770 --> 02:07.730
So let's remove it.

02:07.730 --> 02:09.530
And now we have the correct syntax.

02:09.770 --> 02:11.290
Let's execute it again.

02:11.490 --> 02:17.610
We will get another error because it says in one table you cannot have more than one clustered index.

02:17.610 --> 02:18.730
We have already one.

02:18.730 --> 02:23.010
You have to decide do you want to split your table by columns or by rows?

02:23.210 --> 02:29.210
That's why we have to go and drop the previous index so we can do it like this drop index.

02:29.530 --> 02:32.090
And I need the name of the index like this.

02:32.090 --> 02:34.810
And then we have to specify the table name.

02:37.810 --> 02:38.850
So that's it.

02:38.890 --> 02:40.650
Let's drop the index.

02:40.650 --> 02:47.010
Now if we refresh we cannot see anymore our clustered index and our query should be working.

02:47.010 --> 02:48.130
So let's do that.

02:48.170 --> 02:50.490
Now let's check the indexes again.

02:50.530 --> 02:53.810
And now as you can see we got a new clustered index.

02:53.810 --> 02:55.850
But this time it is columnstore.

02:55.890 --> 03:01.820
Now you can see at the start we have like an icon, it looks like a bar chart or like analytics and

03:01.820 --> 03:02.500
reports.

03:02.500 --> 03:06.780
And that's because the main purpose of creating Columnstore is to have a bar chart.

03:06.820 --> 03:10.740
So now of course we cannot go and create multiple clustered column index.

03:10.740 --> 03:12.900
We can have maximum only one.

03:12.900 --> 03:14.140
So now if you say you know what.

03:14.180 --> 03:17.300
Let's go and create for the first name another index.

03:17.300 --> 03:19.540
But this time it's going to be a column store.

03:19.580 --> 03:27.460
So if I go and copy the whole thing over here and let's say it is none clustered column index and let's

03:27.460 --> 03:29.220
call it for example first name.

03:30.340 --> 03:33.900
And we define over here the first name.

03:34.980 --> 03:36.060
So that's it.

03:36.060 --> 03:37.780
Let's go and execute it.

03:37.780 --> 03:44.780
You will see that we will get an error where a SQL tells us you cannot create multiple columnstore indexes.

03:44.780 --> 03:49.700
That means you can create only one columnstore index for each table.

03:49.700 --> 03:53.820
And you have to decide whether it is a clustered or non-clustered.

03:53.820 --> 03:58.310
And you cannot create like the roster multiple non-clustered index.

03:58.310 --> 04:01.990
So you are allowed only with one columnstore index.

04:01.990 --> 04:04.670
But this limitation is only here in the SQL server.

04:04.790 --> 04:11.750
In other databases, I know that is allowed to use multiple columnstore indexes like in the Azure SQL

04:11.750 --> 04:12.030
server.

04:12.030 --> 04:13.030
You can do that.

04:13.030 --> 04:18.070
So now in order to practice and you would like to create a non-clustered Columnstore index, you can

04:18.070 --> 04:23.270
drop the first one and you can go and create the one that you need as a non-clustered index.

04:23.270 --> 04:24.910
So actually let's go and do that.

04:24.910 --> 04:26.430
Let's drop the first one.

04:26.430 --> 04:27.670
So drop index.

04:28.390 --> 04:32.070
And this is our index on this table.

04:32.110 --> 04:32.870
Let's do that.

04:32.870 --> 04:37.310
And once you execute the non-clustered columnstore index is going to work.

04:37.350 --> 04:43.510
And if you refresh over here you will see that we have a non-clustered Columnstore index for the first

04:43.550 --> 04:43.950
name.

04:44.310 --> 04:44.630
Okay.

04:44.670 --> 04:50.510
So now as we learned that the Columnstore can go and compress the data and the storage that is needed

04:50.510 --> 04:53.990
for the entire table is going to be less than the roster.

04:54.110 --> 04:56.190
So let's see whether that is really true.

04:56.230 --> 05:01.150
Now in order to check this, I will not do that in the database sales DB because everything here is

05:01.150 --> 05:02.030
already small.

05:02.030 --> 05:04.430
We're going to go and use another database.

05:04.430 --> 05:07.590
We have the Adventureworks DW 2022.

05:07.630 --> 05:09.750
And if you have a newer version that's okay.

05:09.750 --> 05:11.110
So now what is the plan.

05:11.150 --> 05:14.830
We're going to go and create three identical copies of one table.

05:14.830 --> 05:16.670
And we're going to have different structures.

05:16.670 --> 05:18.590
So the first one is going to be the heap structure.

05:18.590 --> 05:21.590
The second one is going to be row store structure.

05:21.590 --> 05:23.790
And the third one is going to be column store structure.

05:23.790 --> 05:27.510
And then we're going to go and compare the storage of the three.

05:27.510 --> 05:29.990
So now we have to go and pick one of those tables.

05:29.990 --> 05:31.110
We need one big table.

05:31.110 --> 05:33.430
So for example the fact internet sales.

05:33.430 --> 05:34.750
So let's see how we can do that.

05:34.750 --> 05:36.710
Let's start with the heap structure.

05:36.710 --> 05:40.550
We're going to say select star into a new table.

05:40.550 --> 05:48.270
So it's going to be the facts internet sales and underscore HP for the heap.

05:48.510 --> 05:50.230
And we're going to get it from the table.

05:50.430 --> 05:51.190
Facts.

05:51.520 --> 05:53.800
Internet sales.

05:53.800 --> 05:54.680
So like this.

05:54.680 --> 05:55.640
And here it's very important.

05:55.640 --> 05:58.920
If you are switching databases you have to go and use the database.

05:58.920 --> 06:03.640
So it's going to be use Adventureworks DW 2022.

06:03.680 --> 06:08.600
So execute this at the start to make sure that you are switching to the new database.

06:08.640 --> 06:11.280
And now let's go and execute our heap structure.

06:11.280 --> 06:15.560
So with that we have created heap table as you can see 60,000 rows.

06:15.640 --> 06:20.840
And since we didn't define any clustered index this table is going to be a heap structure.

06:20.880 --> 06:25.880
Now let's go and create another table where we use clustered row store index.

06:25.880 --> 06:32.080
So what we're going to do we're going to copy the whole thing over here I'm going to call this row store.

06:32.240 --> 06:34.880
And we're going to go of course change the name to SES.

06:34.920 --> 06:37.360
But still we are targeting the same table.

06:37.360 --> 06:40.000
So let's go and execute this at the start.

06:40.040 --> 06:44.720
But now in order to make it as clustered row store, we have to go and create an index.

06:44.720 --> 06:46.080
So it's going to be like this.

06:46.680 --> 06:50.080
Create clustered index.

06:50.120 --> 06:53.480
We don't have to specify the roster because it is as a default.

06:53.480 --> 06:54.560
It can be roster.

06:54.600 --> 07:01.480
So let's call it index facts internet sales Rs.

07:01.680 --> 07:03.520
And then the primary key.

07:03.720 --> 07:04.600
So B key.

07:04.640 --> 07:11.320
And now we need the table fact internet sales Rs.

07:11.760 --> 07:14.640
And now we need the columns the primary key.

07:14.760 --> 07:16.640
Well actually I don't know what is the primary key.

07:16.640 --> 07:18.200
So let's go and check that.

07:18.400 --> 07:20.560
So it is a composite primary keys.

07:20.560 --> 07:29.360
So it's going to be the sales order number and sales order line number like this.

07:29.360 --> 07:32.080
So let's go and execute this.

07:32.120 --> 07:34.360
And with that we have clustered row index.

07:34.360 --> 07:36.480
I'm going to go and check what do we have over here.

07:36.480 --> 07:38.120
So let's go and refresh everything.

07:38.120 --> 07:42.440
So we have now two tables the heap and the row store.

07:42.480 --> 07:45.000
So let's extend it and check the indexes.

07:45.040 --> 07:47.760
And as you can see we have the clustered index.

07:47.760 --> 07:49.690
Now we need the third table.

07:49.690 --> 07:51.530
It's going to be the Columnstore index.

07:51.530 --> 07:54.250
I'm just going to go and copy the whole thing over here.

07:54.250 --> 08:01.650
So this is the column store going to be here CSS and CSS.

08:02.450 --> 08:05.450
And of course we don't need any columns for the column store.

08:05.450 --> 08:09.810
And don't forget to add the column store keyword.

08:09.810 --> 08:12.170
So create clustered column store index.

08:12.170 --> 08:14.450
And we have to rename as well over here.

08:14.730 --> 08:16.970
So let's go and execute our new stuff.

08:16.970 --> 08:21.930
So we create first the table and then we convert it to a column store index.

08:21.930 --> 08:23.610
So let's go and do that.

08:23.650 --> 08:26.930
And we have to go and refresh and check our tables.

08:26.930 --> 08:28.850
So this is our third table.

08:28.850 --> 08:30.690
And let's go and check the indexes.

08:30.690 --> 08:32.850
And we have clustered column store.

08:32.890 --> 08:33.330
All right.

08:33.330 --> 08:34.250
So now we are done.

08:34.250 --> 08:36.130
We have our three different tables.

08:36.170 --> 08:39.850
Now let's go and check the storages of those three tables.

08:39.850 --> 08:42.530
So now let's go and check our first table the heap table.

08:42.530 --> 08:45.370
So right click on it and go to the properties.

08:45.410 --> 08:50.100
And now we can see here a lot of informations about our table but we are interested on the storage.

08:50.100 --> 08:52.740
So click here on the page for the storage.

08:52.740 --> 08:55.620
And now we can see here a few informations about the storage.

08:55.620 --> 08:57.460
And one of them is the data space.

08:57.460 --> 09:02.060
It is around nine megabytes and the index space is almost nothing.

09:02.060 --> 09:03.860
So we don't have anything over here.

09:03.860 --> 09:06.100
So this is the storage of the heap structure.

09:06.100 --> 09:07.620
We don't have any indexes.

09:07.660 --> 09:09.980
Let's go now to the row store.

09:10.220 --> 09:13.060
So we're going to go to the SES and properties.

09:13.100 --> 09:14.380
Then let's go to the storage.

09:14.420 --> 09:17.820
And now as you can see the data space is exactly the same.

09:17.860 --> 09:23.300
And that's because whether it is heap or row store index, we're going to store the data in data pages

09:23.300 --> 09:24.140
as rows.

09:24.140 --> 09:27.060
So the size of the data itself will not change.

09:27.060 --> 09:28.660
It will be sorted differently.

09:28.660 --> 09:32.020
But what's changed here is the size of the index.

09:32.020 --> 09:35.380
Now we are consuming more storage for the index.

09:35.380 --> 09:37.860
So that means the overall storage of the table.

09:37.860 --> 09:40.580
With a clustered row store index.

09:40.580 --> 09:42.780
It is more than the heap structure.

09:42.780 --> 09:45.660
Let's go and check now our Columnstore Store index.

09:45.660 --> 09:49.100
So to the CSS and let's go to the properties.

09:49.140 --> 09:52.500
And now it is interesting to see whether our table is getting smaller.

09:52.500 --> 09:53.980
So let's go to the storage.

09:53.980 --> 10:00.020
And as you can see the data space is around one megabyte compared to the nine megabyte.

10:00.060 --> 10:07.380
I know those are small numbers, but still it is massively reduced space because everything is compressed.

10:07.380 --> 10:13.740
And of course we are not using any index spaces because we don't have this B-tree structure in the column

10:13.740 --> 10:14.140
store.

10:14.180 --> 10:17.660
So as you can see, if you compare to the others, it is the winner.

10:17.900 --> 10:23.940
This table that is using the column store is consuming way less storage than the others.

10:23.940 --> 10:30.380
So now if you want to rank it based on the storage, the best one is the column store index table.

10:30.420 --> 10:33.500
Then the next one is the table with the heap structure.

10:33.500 --> 10:38.940
And the worst one is the table with the roster clustered index.

10:39.180 --> 10:39.900
So that's true.

10:39.900 --> 10:44.620
The Columnstore index is consuming less space than the other type of indexes.
